<h1>Prerequisites</h1>
<p>Orcraft is a single <code>pip install</code> on each machine. There is no installer to run as an
  administrator, no system service to configure and no agent to provision - the controller and the
  endpoints are ordinary Python applications that run as your own user.</p>
<p>In practice, a machine that already has Python on it is ready to go. The rest of this page lists
  what Orcraft assumes is in place, and how to satisfy each item in a line or two if it isn't.</p>
<p>Only <strong>three</strong> sections below ask you to do anything. Every heading is marked so you
  can tell at a glance which is which:</p>
<table>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><span class="req req--action req--legend"></span></td>
      <td>Do this to get Orcraft running.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><span class="req req--if req--legend"></span></td>
      <td>Troubleshooting - only when something doesn't behave as expected.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><span class="req req--none req--legend"></span></td>
      <td>Already true on a typical machine. Listed so you know how Orcraft behaves.</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

