<h2 id="operating-systems" class="req req--none">Operating systems</h2>
<p>The controller and endpoints can be run on Windows, Linux and macOS.</p>
<p>Validated on:</p>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>OS</th>
      <th>Version</th>
      <th>Kernel / build</th>
      <th>Architecture</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td>Windows</td>
      <td>10</td>
      <td>build 19045</td>
      <td>x86_64</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Windows</td>
      <td>11</td>
      <td>build 26200</td>
      <td>x86_64</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Ubuntu</td>
      <td>24.04</td>
      <td>kernel 6.17 and 7.0</td>
      <td>x86_64</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>macOS</td>
      <td>26.2</td>
      <td>Darwin 25.2.0</td>
      <td>arm64</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p>Other versions and architectures - arm64 Linux, Windows on Arm, Intel Macs - are expected to
  work wherever Python and PySide6 are available; they simply haven't been through the validation
  matrix above yet.</p>
<p>Windows 10 left mainstream support in October 2025, but it stays in the matrix on purpose: lab
  and instrument machines often can't be upgraded, and endpoints run on it perfectly well.</p>
<p>The controller GUI's best experience is on Windows.</p>

<h3 id="linux-desktop-libraries" class="req req--if">Linux desktop libraries (controller only)</h3>
<p>The controller's GUI is built on Qt, which needs a few shared libraries that desktop Linux
  installs already include. You only need these on a minimal image, where the controller fails to
  start with <code>Could not load the Qt platform plugin "xcb"</code>. Endpoints have no GUI and
  need none of it.</p>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Distribution</th>
      <th>Command</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td>Debian, Ubuntu</td>
      <td>{{ cmd.copyCell("sudo apt install libgl1 libxkbcommon-x11-0 libxcb-cursor0") }}</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Fedora, RHEL</td>
      <td>{{ cmd.copyCell("sudo dnf install mesa-libGL libxkbcommon-x11 xcb-util-cursor") }}</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h2 id="network">Network</h2>
<p>Orcraft uses exactly two things on the network: one TCP port and ping. No additional ports, no
  discovery broadcasts, no inbound connections to the controller.</p>

<h3 id="tcp-port" class="req req--action">TCP port</h3>
<p>Each endpoint listens on <strong>TCP port 50051</strong> and the controller connects out to it.
  Allow that port inbound on the endpoint machine and the connection works.</p>
<table>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <th>Default port</th>
      <td>50051, per endpoint</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <th>Changing it</th>
      <td>Start the endpoint with <code>-port 50052</code>, and enter the same port when adding the
        endpoint in the controller</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <th>Interfaces</th>
      <td>Dual-stack - accepts IPv4 and IPv6. Use <code>-bind</code> to restrict it to one
        interface</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <th>Controller</th>
      <td>Outbound only - no inbound rule of its own</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p>To open the port on the endpoint machine:</p>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>OS</th>
      <th>How</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td>Windows</td>
      <td>{{ cmd.copyCell("New-NetFirewallRule -DisplayName 'Orcraft Endpoint' -Direction Inbound -Protocol TCP -LocalPort 50051 -Action Allow") }}</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Linux</td>
      <td>{{ cmd.copyCell("sudo ufw allow 50051/tcp") }} or the equivalent for
        <code>firewalld</code>/<code>nftables</code></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>macOS</td>
      <td>Doesn't filter by port - allow incoming connections at the prompt the first time you run
        the endpoint</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h3 id="icmp-ping" class="req req--if">ICMP (Ping)</h3>
<p>The controller pings each endpoint to check whether it is reachable, before connecting to the
  endpoint application. Most lab and office networks already allow this. If ping is blocked, allow
  ICMP echo requests between the controller and every endpoint, in both directions:</p>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>OS</th>
      <th>What to allow</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td>Windows</td>
      <td>Inbound "File and Printer Sharing (Echo Request - ICMPv4/ICMPv6)" in Windows Defender
        Firewall, or the equivalent third-party rule</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Linux</td>
      <td>Make sure <code>iptables</code>/<code>nftables</code> - or a front-end such as
        <code>ufw</code> or <code>firewalld</code> - isn't dropping ICMP</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>macOS</td>
      <td>Allow incoming ping in System Settings' firewall, or turn off "Block all incoming
        connections"</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p>Corporate networks, VPNs and cloud security groups often block ICMP by default even when other
  ports are open - check with your network administrator if ping keeps failing.</p>

<h3 id="host-names" class="req req--none">Host names</h3>
<p>Endpoints can be added by IP address, so no DNS setup is required. To add them by host name, the
  name only has to resolve from the controller - a DNS record or a local <code>hosts</code> entry
  both work.</p>

<h2 id="command-shell" class="req req--none">Command shell</h2>
<p>Nothing to install - every supported OS already ships the shell Orcraft uses. Worth knowing which
  one, though, since test steps that run a CLI command are executed by the endpoint's default
  system shell:</p>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Endpoint OS</th>
      <th>Commands run in</th>
      <th>Not</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td>Windows</td>
      <td><code>cmd.exe</code></td>
      <td>PowerShell</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Linux, macOS</td>
      <td><code>/bin/sh</code></td>
      <td>bash or zsh, so their syntax isn't available by default</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p>If a step needs a particular shell, ask for it in the command itself:</p>
{{ codeblock.block('powershell -NoProfile -Command "Get-Process orcraft-endpoint"
bash -c "for i in {1..3}; do ping -c 1 10.0.0.5; done"', 'bash') }}

<h2 id="terminal-output" class="req req--none">Terminal output</h2>
<p>The endpoint and the test framework print colored, formatted output - progress bars, result
  tables, pass/fail rollups - using ANSI escape sequences and Unicode box-drawing characters. Every
  terminal below handles both as they come; the only one that needs attention is Windows' legacy
  console.</p>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Terminal</th>
      <th>Colored output</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td>Windows Terminal, PowerShell 7</td>
      <td>Works as-is</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Windows legacy console (conhost)</td>
      <td>May print raw codes such as <code>&#8592;[92m</code> - see below</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>macOS Terminal.app, iTerm2</td>
      <td>Works as-is</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Linux emulators - gnome-terminal, konsole, xfce4-terminal, kitty, alacritty, xterm</td>
      <td>Works as-is</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p>If a Windows console shows raw escape sequences instead of colors, the simplest fix is to run
  the endpoint from Windows Terminal. To enable it for the classic console instead, set it once per
  user - no administrator rights needed:</p>
{{ cmd.copyCommand("reg add HKCU\\Console /v VirtualTerminalLevel /t REG_DWORD /d 1") }}
<p>If box-drawing characters come out as <code>?</code>, the console is using a legacy code page.
  Windows Terminal uses UTF-8 by default; in the classic console, switch with
  <code>chcp 65001</code>.</p>
<p>None of this affects the controller - its GUI renders the same output itself, with no terminal
  involved.</p>

<h3 id="log-files" class="req req--none">Log files</h3>
<p>Every run is also saved to a <code>.ansi</code> log file with the color codes embedded, so a
  saved log looks exactly like the live output.</p>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Reading a saved log</th>
      <th>How</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td>Controller</td>
      <td>Renders logs directly in its GUI, and opens saved <code>.ansi</code> files</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Linux, macOS</td>
      <td>{{ cmd.copyCell("cat") }} or {{ cmd.copyCell("less -R") }} to scroll with colors intact</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Windows</td>
      <td>{{ cmd.copyCell("Get-Content") }} in Windows Terminal</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Text editors</td>
      <td>Show the escape codes as text unless the editor understands ANSI</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h2 id="permissions" class="req req--none">Permissions</h2>
<p>Orcraft never needs administrator or root access.</p>
<table>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <th>Controller and endpoint</th>
      <td>Run as the logged-in user - no elevation, no system service to install</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <th>Data</th>
      <td>Stored under the user's own profile</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <th>Listening port</th>
      <td>50051 is non-privileged, so binding it needs no special rights</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <th>Test steps</th>
      <td>Inherit the rights of the user running the endpoint - start the endpoint from an elevated
        session if a test needs more</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <th><code>sudo</code> commands</th>
      <td>Only the optional system-package and firewall ones on this page, handled by whoever
        administers the machine</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h2 id="optional-extras">Optional extras</h2>

<h3 id="internet-access">Internet access</h3>
<p>Handy, but not required. Connected machines install with <code>pip install orcraft</code>;
  isolated machines install from a wheel file copied across - see
  <a class="text-accent" href="/setup/getting-started/#install-from-a-wheel-file">Getting started</a>.</p>
<p>Once installed, all test traffic between the controller and its endpoints stays on your own
  network. The controller itself makes two outbound calls, both conveniences that fail quietly and
  never block a run:</p>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Call</th>
      <th>When</th>
      <th>Reaches</th>
      <th>If unavailable</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td>Update check</td>
      <td>Controller startup</td>
      <td><code>pypi.org</code></td>
      <td>Reported as a failed check; nothing is downloaded or installed without you asking</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Suite repository status</td>
      <td>A suite from a Git repository is added to the test explorer</td>
      <td>That repository's own remote</td>
      <td>Status shown as unavailable - see <a class="text-accent" href="#git">Git</a></td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h3 id="git">Git</h3>
<p>Only relevant if your test suites live in Git repositories - which the
  <a class="text-accent" href="/tutorial/">Tutorial</a> suite does. Downloading a suite as an
  archive works just as well, and suites outside a repository skip all of this.</p>
<p>When such a suite is added to the test explorer, the controller runs the <code>git</code> command
  line to show whether your copy is up to date with the repository's remote, and offers a
  fast-forward pull.</p>
<table>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <th>Needs</th>
      <td><code>git</code> on the controller's <code>PATH</code>, and whatever access that remote
        requires - typically the credentials you already use for it</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <th>Behaviour</th>
      <td>Informational only. Runs in the background, never prompts for credentials, never holds up
        a test run</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <th>If unavailable</th>
      <td>The suite's repository status is shown as unavailable; everything else works normally</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h3 id="ai-providers">AI providers</h3>
<p>Orcraft's AI-assisted test generation and analysis are entirely optional - every other feature
  works without them. Endpoints never talk to an AI provider; only the controller does.</p>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Provider</th>
      <th>What you need</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td>OpenAI</td>
      <td>An API key</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Anthropic</td>
      <td>An API key</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Google Gemini</td>
      <td>An API key</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>OpenAI-compatible gateway</td>
      <td>A base URL, plus an API key or OAuth client credentials</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p>Whichever you choose, the controller needs outbound HTTPS to it.</p>
